A||||5||rogergarrison||Tech & Emmisions Guys?||||1||
Z||000000||rogergarrison||06-26-2001||03:22 PM||||I been working on a wrecked Ford Explorer and ran into something ive never seen before. Maybe its same for GMs. On the gas tank filler door is a label that says "Be sure cap is tight or Emmisions/Check Engine lite may come on"  Any ideas on that?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||06-26-2001||03:42 PM||||Yeah, on most late model vehicles, my 99 Grand Am for one, if the gas cap is loose, it'll trip the Service Engine Soon light.  Tightening the cap will cause the light to go off, if that was the cause.  It's to make sure the vapor recovery system is functioning properly.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||06-26-2001||04:05 PM||||It's part of the "OBD-II" emissions systems that are on all cars since about 1997 (Not sure exactly what year it started) to comply with the EPA laws.  Just one more thing to make folks take the car in for service  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tweek||06-26-2001||05:58 PM||||I have heard stories of people going to the dealership to get the ses light turned off, and paying outrageous fees, just for the mechanic to tighted the gas cap.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||06-26-2001||08:14 PM||||As mentioned above this is common for most cars after 97.<P>The tank is actully pressure tested by the car now. If the test fails, bing, on goes the MIL on the dash. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||06-26-2001||08:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tweek:<BR><B>I have heard stories of people going to the dealership to get the ses light turned off, and paying outrageous fees, just for the mechanic to tighted the gas cap.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Some cars if the light is tripped by the gas cap, It'll keep the SES light on until the ECM is reset.<BR>||||reg||1
